PAGE STRIP as a HyperCard-style CARD STACK. Peter recalled HyperCard (Mac, 1987, Bill Atkinson) — its metaphor was a stack of 3x5 index cards, 'a programmable card index', flipped one at a time; the 'tabs with a few lines' feel. Apply to the aifabric-pane PAGE strip: a page-tab should NOT be a cryptic tmux '1:name' — it should be a little CARD showing a headline + a couple of summary lines (keeper/terminal count, unread mail, what's live), i.e. the same summarise-don't-list principle the overview terminal already uses, applied to page navigation. Pages = cards in a stack. tmux's status bar can't do multi-line tabs, BUT we already own a curses surface (the overview terminal) — a multi-line curses PAGE-STRIP is buildable there, making page-flipping feel like flipping labelled cards not reading n:name. Lineage worth honouring: HyperCard influenced the Web (Tim Berners-Lee). Vocabulary: an aifabric-pane > deck > page > terminal.
